A||||9||mshill||Urethane bushings and lubrication...||||
Z||000000||mshill||09-09-1999||06:53 PM||||I just got a whole set of urethane bushings for my 86 SE (a-arms, swaybar, cradle, dogbone).  I have done much research and it seems that it is recommended that you tap the  inner urethane bushing so that it will hold grease.<P>Has anyone installed these without doing so?<BR>Has anyone experienced excessive speaking because they didn't tap them?<P>It just seems like a ton of work, and I don't have a set of taps and I am not sure I want to purchase one either.<P>||||reg||
Z||000001||Patrick||09-09-1999||07:22 PM||||<BR>Just for the heck of it I did a <B>Search</B> for "<B>urethane bushings</B>" and found a ton of info.<P>This <B>Search</B> function is great!<P>||||reg||
Z||000002||RICK||09-09-1999||07:23 PM||||I installed all new bushing to the front of my 87 and installed them according the the instructions using there grease and they do make noise when you hit a bump, I just live with it.||||reg||
Z||000003||lowCG||09-09-1999||07:49 PM||||I used the drill to make a little groove,actually two,around the inside of the bore to hold grease,and then on the ends,where the(control arm)bushings touch the frame,I took the hacksaw,and made a "X" across,about 1/16" deep,packed with grease also.No squeaking yet,but I don't have but a trip around the block on them.||||reg||
Z||000004||Ozzy||09-09-1999||08:21 PM||||I did not knotch, groove or tap my urethane bushings. I made sure that the ends of the bushings are well lubricated with a copper compound and were clean and free of muck.<P>They have been in for 1 month now and so far no squeaks.<P>Ozzy||||reg||
Z||000005||CRAGAR||09-13-1999||02:06 PM||||I've got 6k miles on my urethane bushings, front and rear with no squeaking.  I just followed the directions, without any drilling or fittings.||||reg||1
Z||000006||batboy||09-15-1999||01:33 PM||||I've put poly bushings in an old Chevelle of mine and in a 4x4 truck, both without grease. Both eventually started squeaking a little on bumps. Not bad, but enough for me to follow the tapping procedures and greasing the heck out of my Fiero bushings that I will be installing soon.||||reg||1
Z||000007||lowCG||09-16-1999||02:42 AM||||Hey,Batboy,let me try and save you some hassle on your balljoint install;TIG weld them in place,and maybe even quench the weld with water after,going a quarter inch at a time,to reduce the "pull" that will occur.Failed to do this,and the high amp, wire feed weld job destroyed $150 worth of Caddy balljoints,much to my dismay.<BR>Also will be necesary to raise the front edge of the joint 1/8" or so,to ensure operating clearance.The design has changed in the last few years,used to be more simple.<BR>Have driven the car around with the "new" sloppy balljoints,and with the new springs,and bilsteins,it's still so much nicer.<BR>What's the advertised spring rate on those new springs?  The ones I got are 250#.||||reg||11
Z||000008||batboy||09-16-1999||09:31 PM||||LowCG, thanks for the heads up, but since I found these Suspension Techniques sport springs that are about 1-1/2 lower than stock, I went ahead and bought regular Moog ball joints for Fieros instead. If I need to go lower after it's all installed, I can always do the Caddy thing later. I can't find the spring rate on my new springs though. I might have to email the company to get it. These new springs are a trial and error attempt. They look good though. Haven't started on the suspension yet, I plan to soon. I'll keep you posted. BTW, what is the spring rate on a stock Fiero front spring?||||reg||1
Z||000009||lowCG||09-17-1999||12:17 AM||||Yeah,I've chosen to sell my car to a teenage,drugdealer/sex-offender,will be purchasing a new Porsche soon to replace the tin indian;no more headaches.<BR>Chao.||||reg||1
